        "uuid": "6af61c0f_54a16486",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/connectivity/PermissionMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 378,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-14T06:59:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this is dangerous. An app can circumvent your protections by just having their package name start with this. Package name is entirely up to the app.\n\nBesides the above, I am concerned that this code is setting a bad example and a damaging precedent. We regularly insist that OEMs must not circumvent or detect CTS. I wonder what we should tell an OEM who wants their package added to this list. There is also the case of an OEM listing explicitly CTS package names in their framework code to pass CTS, and who could point to this code and make the point that we\u0027re doing the same thing.\n\nIn our code we use a combination of techniques including adding @TestApi, shell commands, test-only public methods behind hard permission checks etc. I don\u0027t know what the problem you\u0027re trying to solve here is, but if the above doesn\u0027t help I can offer we\u0027d have a quick VC where you can explain the issue and we\u0027d brainstorm and explore options together.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 376,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 378,
        "endChar": 75
      },
      "revId": "2e6f9adb21f67753363e35200e88859bd601a7e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9b1444f3_23780c00",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/connectivity/PermissionMonitor.java",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 882,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-14T06:59:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If I am reading the body of `sendPackagePermissionsForAppId` correctly, this code heavily relies on that method saying that sdkSandboxUid doesn\u0027t have an SDK sandbox and therefore avoiding to send permissions for a sandbox UID, which is pretty confusing. It also overwrites the permissions sent in l.877, which is wasteful. It\u0027s also confusing that this doesn\u0027t have a corresponding block in onPackageRemoved (there could be a comment there to address this point).\n\nISTM that having this code inside sendPackagePermissionsForAppId is most natural, given that method is already looking up the sandbox UID ; since you\u0027ll need to know whether to add or remove it, maybe it\u0027s best to pass it an additional argument for the permissions of the sandbox app ? Or test for UNINSTALLED or something ?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 882,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 882,
        "endChar": 42
      },
      "revId": "2e6f9adb21f67753363e35200e88859bd601a7e7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
